            <ArticleTitle>Acute Alopecia: Evidence to Thallium Poisoning.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>30-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/ijt.ijt_82_16</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Thallium is a toxic heavy metal often involved in criminal poisonings and occasionally in accidental poisoning. Here, we report a case of acute, nonintentional thallium poisoning due to thallium-contaminated alternative medicine for its rarity and to create awareness about the combination of rapid, diffuse alopecia with neurologic and gastrointestinal symptoms among practitioners, professionals, public, and policymakers.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
